<code>assert 0 <= this.indexOf(object) && this.indexOf(object) <= \old(this.size()) => this.contains(object);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= this.indexOf(object) && this.indexOf(object) <= \old(this.size()) => this.contains(object);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \old(this.size()) >= 0 && \old(this.size()) <= this.indexOf(object) => this.contains(object);</code>

However, the natural language assertion seems to imply that if the object is added successfully, then the set should contain the object regardless of the index condition. Therefore, a more accurate assertion would be:

<code>assert \result => this.contains(object);</code>

This assertion reflects that if the `add` method returns `true` (indicating successful addition), then the set should contain the object.
